In this lesson we'll look at how to multiply and divide fractions and decimals with confidence. First up, multiplying fractions. The trick is to cancel any common factors on the top and bottom before you multiply across, which keeps your numbers nice and small. So for example, two-ninths times three-quarters cancels to one over six. Then comes division. Division is just the inverse of multiplication, so you keep the first fraction, change the divide sign to a times sign, and flip the second fraction upside down. People remember it as keep, change, flip. Now for decimals. When you multiply, ignore the decimal points and multiply as if everything were a whole number. Then count the total decimal places across both numbers, and put that many back in your answer. So one-point-four times two-point-five becomes fourteen times twenty-five, which is three-fifty, and two decimal places gives you three-point-five. For division, shift the decimal point in both numbers until the divisor becomes a whole number, then divide as normal. So six-point-three divided by zero-point-one-five becomes six-thirty divided by fifteen, which is forty-two. One quick warning: the word "of" means multiply, so half of forty is twenty. Now let's see it in action.